┌───────────────────────────── 🤖 Agent Started ──────────────────────────────┐
│                                                                             │
│  Agent: Serverless Application Developer                                    │
│                                                                             │
│  Task: Based on storage/blueprint.json, generate a COMPLETE serverless      │
│  application.                                                               │
│                                                                             │
│  Your mission: Generate a COMPLETE serverless application ready for         │
│  deployment.                                                                │
│                                                                             │
│  ============================================================               │
│  PHASE 1: LAMBDA FUNCTIONS                                                  │
│  ============================================================               │
│  For EACH Lambda in blueprint.lambda_functions:                             │
│                                                                             │
│  Step 1.1: Source Code Collection                                           │
│  Primary path: the Blueprint lists source_files for each Lambda — these     │
│  are the                                                                    │
│  files identified by static analysis as directly relevant.                  │
│  1. Always start by using ReadFileTool to read the raw content of every     │
│  file in                                                                    │
│     source_files. This is your primary and most reliable source of truth.   │
│  2. You MAY autonomously invoke CodeRAGTool at any point during code        │
│  transformation                                                             │
│     when you need additional context that ReadFileTool alone cannot         │
│  provide, such as:                                                          │
│     - A helper function or utility called by the source files whose         │
│  definition is not                                                          │
│       in source_files (e.g., "find the definition of                        │
│  validate_booking_date").                                                   │
│     - A dependency whose implementation lives in a file not listed in       │
│  source_files.                                                              │
│     - Understanding how a pattern is used elsewhere in the codebase to      │
│  clarify intent                                                             │
│       (e.g., "how is the payment module called in other route handlers").   │
│     When CodeRAGTool returns promising results, use ReadFileTool to read    │
│  the full                                                                   │
│     file for complete context — do not rely on the RAG snippet alone.       │
│  3. If after reading source_files and querying CodeRAGTool you still lack   │
│  context,                                                                   │
│     use ReadFileTool to read additional files directly (e.g., files         │
│  referenced                                                                 │
│     in imports or called by the source code).                               │
│                                                                             │
│  CRITICAL ANTI-LOOP RULES (STRICT LIMITS ON CODE RAG TOOL):                 │
│  - NEVER call CodeRAGTool twice with the same or a trivially similar        │
│  query.                                                                     │
│    Keep track of every query you have already asked; if you already         │
│  received                                                                   │
│    results for it, ACCEPT those results and move on.                        │
│  - At most 5 CodeRAGTool calls in total for this whole task. CodeRAGTool    │
│  is a                                                                       │
│    supplementary convenience only; the analysis report, blueprint           │
│  source_files                                                               │
│    and ReadFileTool are your primary sources.                               │
│  - If a CodeRAGTool query did not help, fall back to ReadFileTool for the   │
│    specific file path instead of re-querying.                               │
│  - Your mission is to WRITE the handler files. Reading/querying is only a   │
│  means;                                                                     │
│    as soon as you have the source code you need for the current Lambda,     │
│  proceed                                                                    │
│    to Step 1.2 and write its handler file.                                  │
│                                                                             │
│  Step 1.2: Transform to Lambda Handler                                      │
│  Create: output/lambdas/{domain}/{lambda_name}/handler.py (or .js)          │
│  - {domain} is chosen by reasoning from the monolith’s code structure and   │
│  route organization (do not use a rigid parsing rule).                      │
│  - You MAY use output/lambdas/{lambda_name}/handler.py if the monolith has  │
│  no clear domains, but prefer domain folders when sensible.                 │
│  - Example (domainful): output/lambdas/orders/orders-get-order/handler.py   │
│  - Example (flat): output/lambdas/get-item/handler.py                       │
│  - WriteFileTool accepts EXACTLY ONE file per call. Its Action Input MUST   │
│  be one dictionary with keys such as path/content/append/json_key.          │
│  - NEVER pass a list/array of files to WriteFileTool. NEVER batch multiple  │
│  handlers, dependency files, or README sections into one WriteFileTool      │
│  call.                                                                      │
│  - For every generated file, call WriteFileTool separately:                 │
│    one call for one handler, one call for one requirements.txt, one call    │
│  for one package.json, one call for README.md.                              │
│  - If a WriteFileTool call fails, stop and fix that SAME file first. Do     │
│  NOT claim the Lambda was generated and do NOT move on to the next file     │
│  until the current write succeeds.                                          │
│                                                                             │
│  CRITICAL: REFACTOR MINDSET                                                 │
│  Before writing the handler, analyze the responsibility:                    │
│  - Is this a "Worker" (does DB operations directly)? -> Implement business  │
│  logic locally.                                                             │
│  - Is this an "Orchestrator" (coordinates other steps)? -> Use the          │
│  Orchestration Rules below.                                                 │
│                                                                             │
│  Key transformations:                                                       │
│  - Flask/Express routes -> lambda_handler(event, context)                   │
│  - STRICT: Each Lambda implements ONLY ONE endpoint (method+path) from      │
│  entry_points                                                               │
│  - Do NOT implement multiple routes inside one handler (no if/else          │
│  routing)                                                                   │
│  - Extract parameters from event['pathParameters'] /                        │
│  event['queryStringParameters']                                             │
│  - Extract body from json.loads(event['body'])                              │
│  - Replace database calls: SQLite/MySQL -> boto3 DynamoDB client            │
│  - Remove global state (use environment variables)                          │
│                                                                             │
│  CRITICAL: ORCHESTRATION LOGIC RULES                                        │
│  If a Lambda function's primary role is to coordinate a workflow (e.g.,     │
│  create_booking calling flight/payment services):                           │
│                                                                             │
│  1. PURE ORCHESTRATION:                                                     │
│     - It must ONLY invoke other Lambdas via SDK invoke, SQS send_message,   │
│  or EventBridge put_events.                                                 │
│     - It must NOT contain local business logic implementation (e.g., do     │
│  not write reserve_flight() locally).                                       │
│     - It must NOT access the database directly for steps handled by other   │
│  Lambdas.                                                                   │
│                                                                             │
│  2. NO ZOMBIE CODE:                                                         │
│     - Do NOT copy helper functions from the monolith if they are replaced   │
│  by external Lambda calls.                                                  │
│     - If you invoke `invoke_lambda(..., 'reserve_flight')`, DELETE the      │
│  local `reserve_flight` function definition.                                │
│                                                                             │
│  3. ERROR HANDLING:                                                         │
│     - Handle errors from the invoked Lambda payload, not local try/except   │
│  on DB logic.                                                               │
│                                                                             │
│  User Identity Standard Pattern:                                            │
│  ```python                                                                  │
│  def lambda_handler(event, context):                                        │
│      # Get user_id from pre-validated Cognito claims                        │
│      user_id = event['requestContext']['authorizer']['claims']['sub']  #    │
│  UUID                                                                       │
│                                                                             │
│      # If user profile needed, query UserProfiles                           │
│      if need_profile:                                                       │
│          profile = profiles_table.get_item(Key={'userId': user_id})         │
│                                                                             │
│      # Business logic with UUID user_id                                     │
│      return process_logic(user_id, event['body'])                           │
│  ```                                                                        │
│                                                                             │
│  - All userId fields: String type (Cognito Sub UUID)                        │
│  - Add proper error handling and logging                                    │
│                                                                             │
│  Step 1.3: Shared Layer (decide ONCE before writing any handler)            │
│                                                                             │
│  Before writing any handler, scan ALL lambda source_files for repeated      │
│  code.                                                                      │
│                                                                             │
│  Create output/layers/shared/ IF:                                           │
│  - The same utility function is used by 3+ Lambda handlers, OR              │
│  - A heavy 3rd-party dependency is shared by multiple Lambdas.              │
│                                                                             │
│  Layer structure:                                                           │
│  - Python: output/layers/shared/python/shared_utils.py                      │
│             output/layers/shared/requirements.txt  (OPTIONAL — see rules    │
│  below)                                                                     │
│  - Node.js: output/layers/shared/nodejs/shared.js                           │
│             output/layers/shared/nodejs/package.json  (OPTIONAL — see       │
│  rules below)                                                               │
│                                                                             │
│  ⚠️ SAM BUILD BEHAVIOR FOR LAYERS (differs from Lambda functions):          │
│  SAM build treats functions and layers differently:                         │
│  - Lambda functions: SAM auto-detects requirements.txt/package.json in      │
│  CodeUri                                                                    │
│    and runs pip/npm install automatically. No extra config needed.          │
│  - Layers: SAM does NOT auto-install dependencies. Without Metadata:        │
│  BuildMethod,                                                               │
│    SAM just copies ContentUri as-is. The SAM engineer task adds             │
│  BuildMethod to the                                                         │
│    template when a layer exists, which triggers pip/npm install during sam  │
│  build.                                                                     │
│                                                                             │
│  Your responsibility as Code Developer:                                     │
│  - Put custom .py source code in output/layers/shared/python/ (NOT the      │
│  root)                                                                      │
│  - Put requirements.txt (if needed) at output/layers/shared/ (the root)     │
│  - The SAM engineer will configure Metadata: BuildMethod in template.yaml,  │
│    which makes SAM install deps into python/ alongside your source code.    │
│                                                                             │
│  What to include in layer CODE:                                             │
│  - Shared helper functions (response formatters, validators, DynamoDB       │
│  helpers)                                                                   │
│  - Heavy 3rd-party deps shared by 3+ Lambdas                                │
│                                                                             │
│  What NOT to include in layer:                                              │
│  - Business logic specific to one endpoint (keep in that Lambda's handler)  │
│  - boto3 / AWS SDK (already pre-installed in Lambda runtime — do NOT add    │
│  to                                                                         │
│    requirements.txt or package.json, neither for the layer nor for          │
│  individual Lambdas)                                                        │
│                                                                             │
│  LAMBDA RUNTIME BUILT-IN LIBRARIES — NEVER add these to ANY dependency      │
│  file:                                                                      │
│  - Python: boto3, botocore, s3transfer, jmespath, urllib3 (all              │
│  pre-installed)                                                             │
│  - Node.js: Do NOT assume all `@aws-sdk/*` packages are built into          │
│  nodejs22.x.                                                                │
│    CRITICAL exception: `@aws-sdk/lib-dynamodb` is NOT runtime built-in and  │
│  MUST be                                                                    │
│    packaged whenever used (handler code or shared layer). You MAY rely on   │
│  bundled                                                                    │
│    service client packages such as `@aws-sdk/client-sqs` and                │
│  `@aws-sdk/client-dynamodb`,                                                │
│    but helper libraries like `lib-dynamodb` must be explicit dependencies.  │
│  After collecting dependencies from imports, REMOVE all built-in libraries  │
│  above.                                                                     │
│  If the remaining dependency set is EMPTY, DO NOT create requirements.txt   │
│  / package.json                                                             │
│  for the layer. An absent dependency file is correct — SAM will skip        │
│  pip/npm install.                                                           │
│                                                                             │
│  In each Lambda handler that uses the layer:                                │
│  - Python:  from shared_utils import format_response                        │
│  - Node.js: const { formatResponse } = require('/opt/nodejs/shared');       │
│                                                                             │
│  ⚠️ CRITICAL: Node.js layer import compatibility                            │
│  Lambda layers are mounted at /opt/. ESM (import) has strict module         │
│  resolution                                                                 │
│  that does NOT search /opt/ or the Lambda runtime's built-in @aws-sdk/*     │
│  path.                                                                      │
│  CJS (require) uses NODE_PATH which includes /opt/nodejs/ and the           │
│  runtime's                                                                  │
│  built-in modules. Therefore, Node.js Lambda handlers that use shared       │
│  layers                                                                     │
│  MUST use CommonJS require() syntax, NOT ESM import syntax.                 │
│                                                                             │
│  If no shared code is needed, skip this step entirely (do NOT create empty  │
│  layer dirs).                                                               │
│                                                                             │
│  Step 1.4: Cross-Lambda Communication Code                                  │
│                                                                             │
│  There are THREE communication mechanisms. Use the one matching the         │
│  blueprint:                                                                 │
│                                                                             │
│    A) SYNCHRONOUS INVOKE (for entries in                                    │
│  blueprint.lambda_invoke_permissions):                                      │
│    Caller NEEDS the callee's return value for its own HTTP response.        │
│    ```python                                                                │
│    import boto3, json, os                                                   │
│    lambda_client = boto3.client('lambda')                                   │
│    response = lambda_client.invoke(                                         │
│        FunctionName=os.environ['PROCESS_PAYMENT_FUNCTION_NAME'],            │
│        InvocationType='RequestResponse',                                    │
│        Payload=json.dumps({'chargeId': charge_id})                          │
│    )                                                                        │
│    result = json.loads(response['Payload'].read())                          │
│    ```                                                                      │
│                                                                             │
│    B) SQS PUBLISH (for API Lambdas with publishes_to type="sqs"):           │
│    Fire-and-forget: caller does NOT need result. Queue URL from env var.    │
│    ```python                                                                │
│    import boto3, json, os                                                   │
│    sqs = boto3.client('sqs')                                                │
│    sqs.send_message(                                                        │
│        QueueUrl=os.environ['BESTSELLER_QUEUE_URL'],                         │
│        MessageBody=json.dumps({'orderId': order_id, 'books':                │
│  purchased_books})                                                          │
│    )                                                                        │
│    ```                                                                      │
│                                                                             │
│    C) EVENTBRIDGE PUBLISH (for API Lambdas with publishes_to                │
│  type="eventbridge"):                                                       │
│    Event notification: one or more consumers react independently.           │
│    ```python                                                                │
│    import boto3, json                                                       │
│    events = boto3.client('events')                                          │
│    events.put_events(Entries=[{                                             │
│        'Source': 'app.orders',                                              │
│        'DetailType': 'OrderCompleted',                                      │
│        'Detail': json.dumps({'orderId': order_id, 'userId': user_id})       │
│    }])                                                                      │
│    ```                                                                      │
│                                                                             │
│  - Do NOT use HTTP fetch/axios/requests to call another Lambda via API      │
│  Gateway URL.                                                               │
│  - Do NOT use lambda.invoke(InvocationType='Event') — use SQS or            │
│  EventBridge for async.                                                     │
│  - The SAM engineer will add the required IAM policies based on the         │
│  blueprint.                                                                 │
│                                                                             │
│  Step 1.5: Dependencies & Configuration                                     │
│  - REFERENCE analysis_report.json (specifically dependency_graph) and scan  │
│  source code import/require statements to determine dependencies.           │
│  - Do NOT include dependencies already provided by the shared layer (if     │
│  created).                                                                  │
│  - DEPENDENCY FILTERING (apply to EVERY dependency file — layer AND         │
│  individual Lambdas):                                                       │
│    1. Collect raw imports from source code.                                 │
│    2. Remove Python stdlib modules (json, os, decimal, uuid, hashlib,       │
│  etc.).                                                                     │
│    3. Remove Lambda runtime built-ins: boto3, botocore, s3transfer,         │
│  jmespath, urllib3.                                                         │
│       For Node.js, do NOT blanket-remove `@aws-sdk/*`. Keep any SDK         │
│  package that is                                                            │
│       not guaranteed runtime-bundled. `@aws-sdk/lib-dynamodb` must stay as  │
│  a dependency.                                                              │
│    4. Remove packages already provided by the shared layer.                 │
│    5. If the remaining set is EMPTY → DO NOT create a dependency file for   │
│  this Lambda.                                                               │
│       An absent requirements.txt / package.json is correct; SAM will        │
│  simply package                                                             │
│       the handler code as-is, and dependencies come from the runtime +      │
│  shared layer.                                                              │
│  - For Python Lambdas:                                                      │
│    - ONLY create output/lambdas/{lambda_name}/requirements.txt when there   │
│  are                                                                        │
│      non-runtime, non-layer third-party dependencies after filtering.       │
│    - NEVER include boto3, botocore, s3transfer, jmespath — they are         │
│  pre-installed.                                                             │
│  - For Node.js Lambdas:                                                     │
│    - ONLY create output/lambdas/{lambda_name}/package.json when there are   │
│      non-runtime, non-layer third-party dependencies after filtering.       │
│    - DO NOT create requirements.txt for Node.js Lambdas.                    │
│    - AWS SDK Rule: Do NOT assume all `@aws-sdk/*` v3 packages are           │
│  runtime-bundled.                                                           │
│      `@aws-sdk/lib-dynamodb` is NOT built into nodejs22.x and MUST be       │
│  added to                                                                   │
│      package.json or a shared layer package.json when used.                 │
│      Service client packages like `@aws-sdk/client-sqs` and                 │
│      `@aws-sdk/client-dynamodb` may come from the runtime, but it is still  │
│  acceptable                                                                 │
│      to package them if your layer/function directly depends on them.       │
│    - ⚠️ MUST use CommonJS syntax (require/exports) in handler.js — NOT ESM  │
│  (import/export).                                                           │
│      Reason: Lambda layers are mounted at /opt/nodejs/. ESM module          │
│  resolution does NOT                                                        │
│      search NODE_PATH, so `import ... from '/opt/nodejs/shared.js'` fails   │
│  to resolve                                                                 │
│      SDK packages from within layer code. CJS `require()`                   │
│      uses NODE_PATH which correctly includes /opt/nodejs/ and the           │
│  runtime's SDK path.                                                        │
│    - Do NOT add "type": "module" to any package.json. All .js files must    │
│  be treated                                                                 │
│      as CommonJS by the Node.js runtime.                                    │
│    - If package.json is needed (for non-runtime third-party deps), ensure   │
│  it does NOT                                                                │
│      contain "type": "module". Include engines field matching the target    │
│  Node.js runtime.                                                           │
│                                                                             │
│                                                                             │
│                                                                             │
│  ============================================================               │
│  ITERATION BUDGET — DO NOT SELF-LIMIT                                       │
│  ============================================================               │
│  You have 300 tool-call iterations available. That is MORE than enough to   │
│  generate every single Lambda handler. Do NOT worry about "time             │
│  constraints"                                                               │
│  or "running out of iterations". Do NOT create placeholders, stubs, or      │
│  shortcuts. You MUST generate EVERY Lambda handler listed in the blueprint  │
│  with COMPLETE, production-ready business logic. Generate them one by one,  │
│  domain by domain, until ALL are done. There is no time pressure.           │
│                                                                             │
│  ============================================================               │
│  CRITICAL RULES                                                             │
│  ============================================================               │
│  1. PRESERVE ALL BUSINESS LOGIC - do not simplify or skip                   │
│  2. GENERATE COMPLETE FILES - not snippets                                  │
│  3. MAINTAIN DIRECTORY STRUCTURE as shown above                             │
│  4. ENSURE CONSISTENCY - all references between files must work             │
│  5. USE ENVIRONMENT VARIABLES - no hardcoded values                         │
│  5a. WriteFileTool is SINGLE-FILE ONLY:                                     │
│      valid input example -> {"path":                                        │
│  "output/lambdas/orders/orders-create/handler.py", "content": "..."}        │
│      invalid input example -> [{"path": "file1", "content": "..."},         │
│  {"path": "file2", "content": "..."}]                                       │
│      invalid input example -> {"files": [{"path": "file1", "content":       │
│  "..."}]}                                                                   │
│      You MUST write files one at a time.                                    │
│  5b. Before your final answer, ensure every Lambda in                       │
│  blueprint.lambda_functions has a corresponding on-disk handler file under  │
│  output/lambdas/.                                                           │
│      If any handler was not written successfully, continue writing missing  │
│  files instead of reporting success.                                        │
│  6. For SQS-triggered Lambdas (trigger_type="sqs" in blueprint):            │
│     - Handler receives event['Records'], iterate and parse record['body']   │
│     - No API Gateway response format needed (no statusCode/headers/body     │
│  wrapper)                                                                   │
│     - Use batch item failure reporting for partial failures                 │
│  7. For EventBridge-triggered Lambdas (trigger_type="eventbridge" in        │
│  blueprint):                                                                │
│     - Handler receives event['detail'] directly                             │
│     - No API Gateway response format needed                                 │
│  8. For API Lambdas with publishes_to in blueprint:                         │
